## Database: Supabase PostgreSQL

### Overview

The TourWise platform uses Supabase (PostgreSQL) with the following main tables:
- `profiles` - Core user profile data
- `travel_agent` - Agent-specific information
- `itinerary` - User travel itineraries
- `booking` - Trip bookings
- `booking_passengers` - Passenger details for bookings
- `payments` - Payment records for bookings
- `booking_notifications` - Booking-related notifications
- `trips` - Trips offered by agents
- `recommendation` - User reviews/recommendations for trips
- `bus_pooling_requests` - Bus pooling requests between travel agents
- `agent_messages` - Messages between travel agents

---

## Table: `profiles`

**Purpose:** Core user profile information linked to Supabase Auth.

**Columns:**
- `id` (uuid, PRIMARY KEY) - Links directly to `auth.users.id` (Supabase Auth)
- `username` (text) - User's display name
- `preferences` (jsonb) - User-specific settings/preferences (flexible JSON)
- `profile_details` (jsonb) - Additional profile data (flexible JSON)
- `updated_at` (timestamptz) - Last update timestamp

**Relationships:**
- Referenced by `user_id` in: `itinerary`, `booking`, `travel_agent`, `recommendation`
- One-to-one with `auth.users` (via `id`)

**Key Notes:**
- `id` column is the primary key and matches `auth.users.id`
- Use `id` (not `user_id`) when querying profiles table
- `preferences` and `profile_details` are jsonb fields for flexible data storage
- When creating profiles, use `id` from JWT token's `sub` claim

---

## Table: `travel_agent`

**Purpose:** Stores information for users registered as travel agents.

**Columns:**
- `agent_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing agent identifier
- `user_id` (uuid, FOREIGN KEY) - References `profiles.id` or `auth.users.id`
- `name` (text) - Agent's business/display name
- `email` (text) - Agent's contact email
- `verification_status` (verification_status_enum) - Agent verification status (e.g., 'pending', 'verified', 'rejected')
- `contact_info` (jsonb) - Agent contact details (phone, address, etc.)
- `profile_details` (jsonb) - Additional agent-specific profile data
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Account creation timestamp
- `rating` (numeric) - Agent's rating (e.g., 1-5, 1-10)
- `numberofreviews` (int4) - Number of reviews received by the agent

**Relationships:**
- `user_id` → `profiles.id` (many-to-one)
- Referenced by `agent_id` in: `trips`

**Key Notes:**
- Presence in this table determines if user has "agent" role
- To check if user is agent: query `travel_agent` table where `user_id = profiles.id`
- Default `verification_status` is typically 'pending' for new agents
- `rating` and `numberofreviews` are used for displaying top rated agents

---

## Table: `itinerary`

**Purpose:** Stores travel itineraries created by users.

**Columns:**
- `itinerary_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing itinerary identifier
- `user_id` (uuid, FOREIGN KEY) - References `profiles.id` or `auth.users.id`
- `name` (text) - Itinerary name/title
- `start_date` (date) - Trip start date
- `end_date` (date) - Trip end date
- `status` (itinerary_status_enum) - Status enum (e.g., 'planned', 'active', 'completed', 'cancelled')
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Creation timestamp
- `updated_at` (timestamptz) - Last update timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `user_id` → `profiles.id` (many-to-one)
- Referenced by `itinerary_id` in: `booking`

**Key Notes:**
- Users can have multiple itineraries
- Itineraries can contain multiple bookings
- `updated_at` tracks when itinerary was last modified

---

## Table: `trips`

**Purpose:** Trips offered by travel agents with specific origin, destination, and transport details.

**Columns:**
- `trip_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing trip identifier
- `agent_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `travel_agent.agent_id`
- `origin_city` (text) - Origin city of the trip
- `destination_province` (text) - Destination province/state
- `destination_city` (text) - Destination city
- `departure_time` (timestamptz) - Scheduled departure time
- `arrival_time` (timestamptz) - Scheduled arrival time
- `price` (numeric) - Trip price
- `transport_type` (service_type_enum) - Type of transportation (e.g., 'flight', 'bus', 'train', 'car_rental', 'tour', 'transport')
- `total_seats` (int4) - Total number of seats available
- `available_seats` (int4) - Number of seats currently available
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Trip creation timestamp
- `suitability` (suitability_enum) - Suitability for different traveler types (e.g., 'Solo Travelers', 'Families', 'Couples')
- `image_url` (text) - URL to the trip's main image

**Relationships:**
- `agent_id` → `travel_agent.agent_id` (many-to-one)
- Referenced by `trip_id` in: `booking`, `recommendation`

**Key Notes:**
- Each trip belongs to one agent
- `transport_type` enum categorizes the transportation method
- `suitability` enum indicates which traveler types the trip is suitable for
- Seat availability is tracked with `total_seats` and `available_seats`
- Trips have specific location fields (`origin_city`, `destination_city`, `destination_province`) for filtering and search
- `image_url` stores the URL to the trip's main image (typically stored in Supabase Storage)

---

## Table: `booking`

**Purpose:** Records bookings made by users for trips.

**Columns:**
- `booking_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing booking identifier
- `user_id` (uuid, FOREIGN KEY) - References `profiles.id` or `auth.users.id`
- `trip_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `trips.trip_id`
- `itinerary_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY, nullable) - References `itinerary.itinerary_id` (optional, bookings can be standalone)
- `booking_date` (timestamptz) - When booking was made
- `status` (booking_status_enum) - Booking status enum (e.g., 'pending', 'confirmed', 'cancelled', 'completed', 'refunded', 'expired')
- `payment_info` (jsonb) - Payment-related details (legacy field, use `payments` table for detailed payment tracking)
- `number_of_seats` (int4) - Number of seats booked (must be > 0)
- `total_price` (numeric) - Total price for the booking (price per seat × number of seats)
- `passenger_names` (text[]) - Array of passenger names (quick reference)
- `contact_email` (text) - Contact email for the booking
- `contact_phone` (text) - Contact phone number for the booking
- `special_requests` (text) - Special requests or notes from the traveler
- `booking_reference` (text, UNIQUE) - Unique booking reference code (e.g., "TW-2025-001234")
- `confirmed_at` (timestamptz, nullable) - When booking was confirmed by agent
- `cancelled_at` (timestamptz, nullable) - When booking was cancelled
- `cancellation_reason` (text, nullable) - Reason for cancellation
- `refund_amount` (numeric, nullable) - Refund amount if booking was cancelled
- `updated_at` (timestamptz) - Last update timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `user_id` → `profiles.id` (many-to-one)
- `trip_id` → `trips.trip_id` (many-to-one)
- `itinerary_id` → `itinerary.itinerary_id` (many-to-one, nullable)
- Referenced by `booking_id` in: `booking_passengers`, `payments`, `booking_notifications`

**Key Notes:**
- Bookings link users, trips, and optionally itineraries
- `itinerary_id` is nullable - bookings can be standalone or part of an itinerary
- `number_of_seats` must be positive (constraint: `number_of_seats > 0`)
- `total_price` must be non-negative (constraint: `total_price >= 0`)
- `booking_reference` is unique and used for booking lookup
- `passenger_names` is an array for quick reference; detailed passenger info is in `booking_passengers` table
- `payment_info` is legacy; use `payments` table for detailed payment tracking
- Status workflow: pending → confirmed → completed, or pending → cancelled → refunded

---

## Table: `booking_passengers`

**Purpose:** Stores detailed passenger information for each booking.

**Columns:**
- `passenger_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing passenger identifier
- `booking_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `booking.booking_id`
- `full_name` (text) - Passenger's full name
- `age` (int4, nullable) - Passenger's age
- `gender` (text, nullable) - Passenger's gender ('male', 'female', 'other', or NULL)
- `passport_number` (text, nullable) - Passport number for international travel
- `emergency_contact_name` (text, nullable) - Emergency contact person's name
- `emergency_contact_phone` (text, nullable) - Emergency contact phone number
- `dietary_restrictions` (text, nullable) - Dietary restrictions or preferences
- `medical_conditions` (text, nullable) - Medical conditions or special needs
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Record creation timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `booking_id` → `booking.booking_id` (many-to-one)

**Key Notes:**
- Multiple passengers can be associated with a single booking
- Passenger details are optional but recommended for trip planning
- Emergency contact information is important for safety
- Dietary and medical information helps agents prepare appropriately

---

## Table: `payments`

**Purpose:** Tracks payment transactions for bookings.

**Columns:**
- `payment_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing payment identifier
- `booking_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `booking.booking_id`
- `amount` (numeric) - Payment amount (must be > 0)
- `currency` (text) - Currency code (default: 'USD')
- `payment_method` (text) - Payment method ('card', 'bank_transfer', 'cash', 'paypal', etc.)
- `payment_status` (text) - Payment status ('pending', 'completed', 'failed', 'refunded')
- `transaction_id` (text, UNIQUE, nullable) - External payment gateway transaction ID
- `payment_date` (timestamptz, nullable) - When payment was processed
- `refunded_at` (timestamptz, nullable) - When payment was refunded
- `refund_amount` (numeric, nullable) - Refund amount if payment was refunded
- `payment_details` (jsonb, nullable) - Additional payment gateway response data
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Payment record creation timestamp
- `updated_at` (timestamptz) - Last update timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `booking_id` → `booking.booking_id` (many-to-one)

**Key Notes:**
- Multiple payments can be associated with a single booking (partial payments, refunds)
- `amount` must be positive (constraint: `amount > 0`)
- `transaction_id` is unique and links to external payment gateway
- `payment_details` jsonb stores gateway-specific response data
- Payment status workflow: pending → completed, or pending → failed, or completed → refunded

---

## Table: `booking_notifications`

**Purpose:** Stores booking-related notifications for users.

**Columns:**
- `notification_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing notification identifier
- `booking_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `booking.booking_id`
- `user_id` (uuid, FOREIGN KEY) - References `profiles.id` or `auth.users.id`
- `notification_type` (text) - Type of notification ('booking_confirmed', 'payment_received', 'trip_reminder', 'cancellation', 'refund_processed', etc.)
- `title` (text) - Notification title
- `message` (text) - Notification message content
- `is_read` (boolean) - Whether notification has been read by user (default: false)
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Notification creation timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `booking_id` → `booking.booking_id` (many-to-one)
- `user_id` → `profiles.id` (many-to-one)

**Key Notes:**
- Notifications are created for various booking events
- `is_read` flag tracks read status for unread notification counts
- Notifications can be filtered by `user_id` and `is_read` for efficient querying
- Used for in-app notifications and can be extended for email/push notifications

---

## Table: `recommendation`

**Purpose:** User reviews/recommendations for trips.

**Columns:**
- `recommendation_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing recommendation identifier
- `user_id` (uuid, FOREIGN KEY) - References `profiles.id` or `auth.users.id`
- `trip_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `trips.trip_id`
- `score` (numeric) - Rating score (e.g., 1-5, 1-10)
- `reasoning` (text) - Textual review or reason for the score
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Review creation timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `user_id` → `profiles.id` (many-to-one)
- `trip_id` → `trips.trip_id` (many-to-one)

**Key Notes:**
- Users can review trips they've booked/used
- Multiple recommendations per trip (aggregate for ratings)

---

## Table: `bus_pooling_requests`

**Purpose:** Manages bus pooling requests between travel agents for matching trips.

**Columns:**
- `request_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing request identifier
- `requester_agent_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `travel_agent.agent_id` (agent making the request)
- `target_agent_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `travel_agent.agent_id` (agent receiving the request)
- `requester_trip_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `trips.trip_id` (requester's trip)
- `target_trip_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `trips.trip_id` (target agent's trip)
- `status` (pooling_status_enum) - Request status: 'pending', 'approved', 'rejected', 'active', 'completed', 'cancelled'
- `message` (text) - Optional message from requester
- `seat_management` (text) - Seat management option: 'combined' or 'separate'
- `selected_bus_agent_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `travel_agent.agent_id` (which agent's bus to use, set when approved)
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Request creation timestamp
- `updated_at` (timestamptz) - Last update timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `requester_agent_id` → `travel_agent.agent_id` (many-to-one)
- `target_agent_id` → `travel_agent.agent_id` (many-to-one)
- `requester_trip_id` → `trips.trip_id` (many-to-one)
- `target_trip_id` → `trips.trip_id` (many-to-one)
- `selected_bus_agent_id` → `travel_agent.agent_id` (many-to-one, nullable)

**Key Notes:**
- Agents cannot create pooling requests with themselves
- Trips must match: same origin_city, destination_city, same date (any time), same suitability
- Status workflow: pending → approved/rejected → active → completed
- `seat_management` determines if seats are combined or managed separately
- `selected_bus_agent_id` indicates which agent's bus will be used (set during approval)

---

## Table: `agent_messages`

**Purpose:** Stores messages between travel agents for collaboration and communication.

**Columns:**
- `message_id` (int8, PRIMARY KEY) - Auto-incrementing message identifier
- `sender_agent_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `travel_agent.agent_id` (sender)
- `receiver_agent_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `travel_agent.agent_id` (receiver)
- `subject` (text) - Message subject/title
- `content` (text) - Message content
- `related_pooling_request_id` (int8, FOREIGN KEY) - References `bus_pooling_requests.request_id` (optional, links message to pooling request)
- `is_read` (boolean) - Whether message has been read by receiver
- `created_at` (timestamptz) - Message creation timestamp

**Relationships:**
- `sender_agent_id` → `travel_agent.agent_id` (many-to-one)
- `receiver_agent_id` → `travel_agent.agent_id` (many-to-one)
- `related_pooling_request_id` → `bus_pooling_requests.request_id` (many-to-one, nullable)

**Key Notes:**
- Agents cannot send messages to themselves
- Messages can be linked to pooling requests for context
- `is_read` flag tracks read status for notifications

